;; Docs for std/random.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "random.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Random numbers: ChaCha20, one generator per thread, and sources of your own.")
  (see random-int current-random make-random with-random-seed)
  (example """
    (import (std random))

    (random-int 1 6)                          ; the calling thread's generator
    (random-int 1 6 #:rng my-source)          ; one of your own
    (with-random-seed 42UL (deal-a-hand))     ; a run that replays
    """)
  (reference """
    The generator is ChaCha20, seeded with 256 bits from the operating
    system's CSPRNG, one per thread. The default path locks nothing and
    shares nothing, so drawing from two threads at once costs what drawing
    from one costs. A source you make yourself is locked instead, once per
    operation.

    Every function takes @code{#:rng}, which defaults to
    @code{(parameter-ref current-random)}. A program that wants another
    source binds the parameter and changes no call site.

    @strong{The ambient source.} The value of @code{current-random} holds no generator. It names the
    calling thread's, and which thread that is is decided at each draw.
    That is what makes the default safe on a bjoroutine: a fiber may resume
    on a thread it did not suspend on, and a source read before the
    suspension would otherwise go on drawing from a generator another fiber
    is already inside. The ambient source has no state to race on.

    @strong{A bound source is safe to share, and still does not replay.} Every
    operation on a source you made takes its lock, so no draw is lost,
    doubled or torn however fibers interleave. What the lock cannot give is
    what the seed was for: the stream is one sequence, but which fiber gets
    which value of it is the scheduler's choice.

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (parameterize ((current-random (make-random/seed64 42UL)))
      (bjo (worker))     ; these two share one generator, under its lock:
      (bjo (worker)))    ; safe, but the order they draw in is not fixed
    }|

    Give each fiber @code{(random-split (parameter-ref current-random))}, or
    a seed of its own, and the run replays. The default source needs
    neither: it is one generator per thread, reached by no other.

    @strong{The lock is per operation, not per draw.} @code{shuffle-array!}
    and @code{random-bytes!} are many draws and take the lock once, so a
    shuffle is the permutation the stream describes rather than one
    interleaved with another fiber's. It also means that shuffling a large
    array under a source other fibers are drawing from makes them wait.

    @strong{Binding a seeded source makes everything under it predictable},
    including code you did not have in mind: a session token minted inside a
    @code{with-random-seed} is guessable. Keep the extent to what is being
    replayed.

    @strong{Seeded means reproducible, not unguessable.}
    @code{make-random/seed64} and @code{make-random/seed256} take what they
    are given. Use @code{make-random}, or the default, for anything anyone
    else must not predict.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Sources
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc RandomSource
  (summary "Where random numbers come from: the ambient source, or a generator of its own.")
  (see current-random make-random random-split)
  (reference """
    A source made by @code{make-random} and its neighbours holds a generator,
    and every operation on it takes its lock. The ambient source, the default
    value of @code{current-random}, holds none and draws from the calling
    thread's.
    """))

(:doc current-random
  (summary "The source every function draws from when it is not given #:rng.")
  (see with-random-seed RandomSource)
  (example """
    (parameterize ((current-random (make-random/seed64 7UL)))
      (random-int 1 6))   ; the same number every run
    """)
  (reference """
    A @code{(Param RandomSource)}. Its default value is the ambient source,
    which draws from the calling thread's generator at each draw, so it is
    safe to read on a bjoroutine and to hand to another thread.
    """))

(:doc make-random
  (summary "A new generator, seeded with 256 bits from the operating system.")
  (returns "A source of its own: unpredictable, and locked per operation.")
  (see make-random/seed64 random-split))

(:doc make-random/seed64
  (summary "A generator that replays: the same seed draws the same numbers.")
  (arg seed "Any 64 bits.")
  (returns "A source of its own, locked per operation.")
  (see make-random/seed256 with-random-seed)
  (reference """
    The 64 bits are stretched to ChaCha20's 256-bit key by SplitMix64, so
    only 2@sup{64} of the generators are reachable. Reproducible is not
    unguessable: use @code{make-random} for anything that must not be
    predicted.
    """))

(:doc make-random/seed256
  (summary "A generator that replays, seeded at ChaCha20's full key width.")
  (arg a "The first 64 bits of the key.")
  (arg b "The next 64.")
  (arg c "The next 64.")
  (arg d "The last 64. Four words make the key, so no seed is malformed.")
  (returns "A source of its own, locked per operation.")
  (see make-random/seed64))

(:doc random-split
  (summary "An independent generator, drawn from another source.")
  (arg r "The source to draw the new generator's key from. On the ambient source, the calling thread's generator.")
  (returns "A source of its own.")
  (see make-random with-random-seed)
  (example """
    (def mine (random-split (parameter-ref current-random)))
    (bjo (worker #:rng mine))
    """)
  (reference """
    How a fiber gets a generator of its own: split one per fiber from a
    seeded source and the run replays however the fibers are scheduled.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Drawing
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc random-int
  (summary "A number from lo to hi, both included.")
  (arg lo "The least it may answer.")
  (arg hi "The most it may answer. Not less than lo.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "A number in [lo, hi], each equally likely.")
  (raises System.ArgumentException "When hi is less than lo.")
  (see random-ref random-double)
  (example """
    (random-int 1 6)   ; a die
    """)
  (reference """
    Lemire's method, so there is no modulo bias. Mind that @code{hi} is
    included: @code{(random-int 0 (vec-length v))} can be one past the end,
    and @code{random-ref} is the way to pick an element.
    """))

(:doc random-double
  (summary "A number from 0, included, to 1, not included.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "A double in [0, 1), with 53 bits of randomness.")
  (see random-int random-chance?))

(:doc random-u32
  (summary "32 random bits.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "A uint, every value equally likely.")
  (see random-u64 random-bytes!))

(:doc random-u64
  (summary "64 random bits.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "A ulong, every value equally likely.")
  (see random-u32 random-bytes!))

(:doc random-bool
  (summary "#t or #f, equally likely.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "One random bit. They are drawn 64 at a time.")
  (see random-chance?))

(:doc random-chance?
  (summary "#t with probability p.")
  (arg p "How likely #t is, from 0 to 1.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "#t with probability p. Outside [0, 1] it is the nearer of #f and #t, and costs no draw.")
  (see random-bool random-double)
  (example """
    (when (random-chance? 0.01) (log-sample request))
    """))

(:doc random-bytes!
  (summary "Fills an array with random bytes, in place.")
  (arg bytes "The array to fill.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (see random-u64)
  (reference """
    Whole blocks of the generator's output go straight into the array, so
    this is the way to ask for many bytes at once. A source of its own is
    locked once for the whole fill.
    """))

(:doc shuffle-array!
  (summary "Shuffles an array in place.")
  (arg a "The array, which is shuffled.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (see shuffle-vec)
  (reference """
    Fisher-Yates. It answers nothing: the array it was given is the shuffled
    one. A source of its own is locked once for the whole shuffle.
    """))

(:doc shuffle-vec
  (summary "A shuffled copy of a vec.")
  (arg v "The vec, which is left as it was.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "A new vec with v's elements in random order.")
  (see shuffle-array!)
  (reference """
    A @code{Vec} is persistent, so unlike @code{shuffle-array!} this answers
    a new one.
    """))

(:doc random-ref
  (summary "An element of a vec, each equally likely.")
  (arg v "The vec to pick from. Not empty.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "One of v's elements.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When v is empty.")
  (see random-int shuffle-vec)
  (example """
    (random-ref ["rock" "paper" "scissors"])
    """)
  (reference """
    Only for a @code{Vec}: picking from any collection would need
    @code{Refable}'s index pinned to @code{int}, which a @code{where} clause
    cannot say.
    """))

(:doc random-string
  (summary "A random string over A-Z, a-z, 0-9, - and _.")
  (arg length "How many characters.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "A string of that many characters, each of the 64 equally likely.")
  (raises System.ArgumentOutOfRangeException "When length is negative.")
  (see random-string/chars random-bytes!)
  (reference """
    Six bits per character, which suits a token in a URL. Draw it from the
    default source or from @code{make-random}, never under a seed, when it
    must not be guessed.
    """))

(:doc random-string/chars
  (summary "A random string over an alphabet of your own.")
  (arg length "How many characters.")
  (arg alphabet "The characters to pick from, each equally likely. Not empty.")
  (key #:rng "The source to draw from.")
  (returns "A string of that many characters from alphabet.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When alphabet is empty, or length is negative.")
  (see random-string)
  (example """
    (random-string/chars 6 (string->vec "0123456789"))   ; a PIN
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Binding a source
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc with-random-seed
  (summary "Runs body with a generator that replays.")
  (form (with-random-seed seed body ...))
  (arg seed "A ulong; the same seed draws the same numbers.")
  (rest body "What to run with it.")
  (see current-random make-random/seed64 random-split)
  (example """
    (with-random-seed 42UL
      (deal-a-hand))   ; the same hand every run
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{current-random} bound to @code{(make-random/seed64 seed)} for
    body's extent. That is one generator for the whole extent: a fiber
    spawned in it shares it, safely, but draws in the scheduler's order, so
    the run stops replaying. A fiber wants @code{random-split} or a seed of
    its own.
    """))
